          %title Cantors Theorem
          %h1#cantors_theorem Cantor's Theorem
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#summary"} Summary
              %li
                %a{:href => "#history"} History
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statements_and_proofs"} Statements and proofs
              %li
                %a{:href => "#interpretation"} Interpretation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#in_posets"} In posets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#summary Summary
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
            proved many theorems, but the one usually called
            %em Cantor's theorem
            is the first nontrivial theorem of Cantor's new
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
          %p
            (The theorem applies to all sets, not just infinite ones, although it's fairly obvious for
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
          %p
            Cantor's theorem should not be confused with the Cantor–Schroeder–Bernstein theorem (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
          %h2#history History
          %p
            Prior to Cantor, people tended to think of infinity (whether they believed in it or not) as an absolute concept: all infinities are equivalent. It had been noticed (by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galileo+Galilei"} Galileo
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite set
            a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-function"} self
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
            that is not a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            of even integers and the infinity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            of all integers— that are actually equivalent, even though at first glance it would appear that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is smaller.
          %p
            Cantor showed that such an equivalence
            %em fails
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            can be surjective (so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uncountable+set"} uncountable
            %em diagonal argument
            to show that no map from any set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}S
            could be surjective. (This covered the uncountability of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}N
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor%E2%80%93Schr%C3%B6der%E2%80%93Bernstein+Theorem"} Cantor–Schröder–Bernstein Theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
            map) from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}S
          %p
            Cantor's argument, like his whole set theory, was controversial at the time. Those who, like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leopold+Kronecker"} Kronecker
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+mathematics"} finite mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructivists
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jan+Brouwer"} Brouwer
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"} Weyl
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}N
            is not constructively valid, although Cantor's original proof that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is uncountable can be made to work.)
          %p
            However, the versions of the theorem stated below are constructively valid, and Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lawvere"}
            is even
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicatively
            valid (as long as one has
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal language
            of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            (and Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lawvere"}
            is a theorem in any
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
          %h2#statements_and_proofs Statements and proofs
          #lawvere.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p (Lawvere's version.)
            %p
              Given sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
